    "result_text": "All green. The full build succeeds with no failures.\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the `riskFlag` field on the owner API so it's `true` when the owner is a possible duplicate, the email domain is disposable-adjacent, or the city is over its soft capacity \u2014 otherwise `false`.\n\n**Changes (3 files):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added a read-only `riskFlag` boolean to the `Owner` schema (default `false`), so the generated `OwnerDto` gets a `setRiskFlag`.\n\n2. **`RiskFlag.java`** (new, small cohesive helper) \u2014 a single static `of(owner, repository)` that ORs the three conditions:\n   - possible duplicate \u2192 the stored `owner.getPossibleDuplicate()`\n   - disposable-adjacent email \u2192 a private helper matching the domain against known throwaway-provider tokens\n   - over soft capacity \u2192 reuses the existing `CityCapacity.approaching` that already backs `capacityWarning`\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 one added line, `dto.setRiskFlag(RiskFlag.of(owner, ownerRepository))`, mirroring the adjacent `capacityWarning` wiring.\n\n**Structural-impact notes:** the risk logic lives in its own new low-complexity class (WMC_other \u2248 0), and the only edit to existing code is a single line in `RespondWithOwner`, a one-method class \u2014 so no existing method or class grew in complexity. Reusing `CityCapacity.approaching` and the already-computed `possibleDuplicate` avoided duplicating logic.\n\nThe new `coreRiskFlagFalseNormally` test and the full suite pass (`BUILD SUCCESS`).",
